---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <SEO
      title="Documentation · CHIND"
      description="Interface guide and reading manual for the CHIND digital edition."
      siteMetadata={siteMetadata}
    />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href={withBase('/images/favicon.png')} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <ChindTopNav />

    <main class="doc-page">
      <div class="doc-shell">
        <aside class="doc-sidebar">
          <h2>Documentation</h2>
          <p>
            A compact guide to the CHIND interface, with annotated figures and short explanations for each main section.
          </p>

          <nav aria-label="Documentation navigation">
            <ul>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#introduction">Introduction</a>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#home">Home</a>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#text-comparison">Text comparison</a>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#analysis">Analysis</a>
                <ul class="doc-nav-sub">
                  <li><a href="#analysis-filters">Filter bar</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#analysis-note">Analysis Note</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#analysis-overview">Overview</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#analysis-explore">Explore words</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#analysis-compare">Compare sections</a></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#dictionary">Dictionary</a>
                <ul class="doc-nav-sub">
                  <li><a href="#dictionary-page">Reading a dictionary page</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#dictionary-row">Reading a dictionary row</a></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#index">Index</a>
                <ul class="doc-nav-sub">
                  <li><a href="#index-overview">General layout</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#index-table">Results table</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#index-analytics">Analytics bar</a></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#character-detail">Character detail</a>
                <ul class="doc-nav-sub">
                  <li><a href="#character-top">Upper section</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#character-bottom">Lower section</a></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li class="doc-nav-item">
                <a href="#credits">Credits</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>

        <article class="doc-content">
          <header class="doc-hero" id="introduction">
            <span class="doc-kicker">Guide</span>
            <h1>Using the CHIND interface</h1>
            <p>
              This section explains how to navigate the digital environment dedicated to Basilio Brollo’s <em>Dictionarium sinico-latinum</em>.
              The documentation is organised by page type: Home, Text Comparison, Analysis, Dictionary, Index, Character Detail, and Credits.
              Each figure below is interactive: the numbered labels open the corresponding explanatory note.
            </p>
            <div class="doc-intro-grid">
              <div class="doc-intro-card">
                <strong>How to use this page</strong>
                <span>Start with a section in the left sidebar, then follow the numbered annotations attached to each screenshot.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-intro-card">
                <strong>What changes with filters</strong>
                <span>Most tables and charts are dynamic. Counts, loci and distributions will change according to the active query.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-intro-card">
                <strong>About the screenshots</strong>
                <span>The examples are illustrative. The layout is stable, but the exact data visible in the screenshots can vary over time.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </header>

          <section class="doc-section" id="home">
            <h2>Home</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The home page provides a concise introduction to the project and foregrounds the main access points to the digital edition.
              It also works as a thematic landing page, connecting the research framework, the historical context of Basilio Brollo,
              and the main navigational branches of the website.
            </p>
            <div class="doc-simple-grid">
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Project presentation</h3>
                <p>
                  The introductory blocks summarise the CHIN-DICTIONARY project, the historical importance of the manuscript,
                  and the broader scholarly aims of the digital edition.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Gateway to the edition</h3>
                <p>
                  The top navigation bar is shared across the site. From the home page the reader can quickly move to Documentation,
                  Text Comparison, the Character Index, the Dictionary browser, and the Credits page.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="text-comparison">
            <h2>Text comparison</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The Text Comparison tool allows an external Chinese text to be checked against the lexicographic material represented in CHIND.
              Users can paste text directly or upload a supported file, then inspect which characters are directly attested in the manuscript,
              represented through a linked historical glyph, documented only as occurrence-backed graphic variants, or absent from the current dataset.
              An optional mode also identifies two-character units recorded in <code>composite_words</code>.
            </p>
            <div class="doc-simple-grid">
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Reading the comparison</h3>
                <p>
                  Colour-coded highlights are applied directly to the submitted text. Clicking a recognised character or compound updates the Scholarly Inspector,
                  where the reader can review readings, attestation counts, documentary evidence, and links to the full Character Record or the relevant Dictionary locus.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Long texts and optional modes</h3>
                <p>
                  Long texts can be browsed in manageable blocks, while the text and inspector use independent scrolling areas so that contextual information remains visible during close reading.
                  Historical romanisation and disyllabic matching can be enabled independently according to the purpose of the comparison.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="analysis">
            <h2>Latin lexical analysis</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The Analysis page provides an exploratory interface for the Latin text stored in the dictionary definitions.
              The figures below use the same numbered guide adopted elsewhere in this documentation: click a label on the screenshot to jump to the related instruction.
            </p>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="analysis-filters">
              <h3>Common controls · Filter bar</h3>
              <p>The filter bar defines the corpus used by all three analysis tabs.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/analysis-filter-bar.png')}
                alt="Annotated filter bar of the Latin lexical analysis interface"
                caption="The common filter bar controls where to search, what to group, and which forms to exclude."
                callouts={analysisFilterCallouts}
                leftGutter="72px"
                rightGutter="104px"
                topGutter="102px"
                bottomGutter="112px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Corpus</h4>
                  <p>Choose the general corpus scope before applying more specific filters.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Page range</h4>
                  <p>Set the first and last dictionary page to restrict the analysis to a continuous range.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Typology</h4>
                  <p>Limit the corpus to one occurrence typology, or leave all typologies active.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Word groups</h4>
                  <p><strong>Edit groups</strong> creates or changes user-defined groups of forms that can be followed together.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Function words</h4>
                  <p>Function words are excluded by default. Switch this control on when they are part of the research question.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-6">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">6</span> Word exclusions</h4>
                  <p><strong>Edit exclusions</strong> adds or removes user-defined forms from the analytical corpus.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-7">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">7</span> Apply filters</h4>
                  <p>Press <strong>Apply filters</strong> after changing the corpus controls to rebuild the current analytical view.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-filter-8">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">8</span> Cleaning audit</h4>
                  <p><strong>Review text cleaning</strong> opens the list of excluded or cleaned tokens for a quick quality check.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="analysis-note">
              <h3>Common controls · Analysis Note</h3>
              <p>The Analysis Note gives the methodological context for the currently selected chart or table.</p>
              <div class="doc-analysis-figure-narrow">
                <DocumentationFigure
                  src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/analysis-info-page.png')}
                  alt="Annotated Analysis Note sidebar"
                  caption="The information panel explains how to interpret a result and provides links back to evidence and documentation."
                  callouts={analysisNoteCallouts}
                  leftGutter="172px"
                  rightGutter="172px"
                  topGutter="34px"
                  bottomGutter="40px"
                />
              </div>
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Close panel</h4>
                  <p>Close the sidebar without changing the selected analysis or active filters.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Purpose</h4>
                  <p>A short statement of the question the selected analysis is designed to answer.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Method</h4>
                  <p>Shows the calculation or counting rule used for the current result.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> How to read</h4>
                  <p>Provides the main interpretative cautions. Use this panel for detail rather than relying on the graph alone.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> References</h4>
                  <p>Lists the methodological sources associated with the selected analysis.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-6">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">6</span> Open documentation</h4>
                  <p>Returns to the relevant documentation section for interface-level guidance.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-note-7">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">7</span> View evidence</h4>
                  <p>When available, opens the underlying attestations behind the numerical result.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="analysis-overview">
              <h3>Overview</h3>
              <p>Overview gives a quick description of the currently filtered Latin corpus.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/analysis-overview.png')}
                alt="Annotated Overview tab of the Latin lexical analysis dashboard"
                caption="Overview combines corpus totals, frequency ranking, distribution across pages, and definition-length descriptors."
                callouts={analysisOverviewCallouts}
                leftGutter="112px"
                rightGutter="126px"
                topGutter="72px"
                bottomGutter="54px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-overview-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Corpus counters</h4>
                  <p>These cards summarise accepted tokens, distinct forms, analysable entries and hapax forms.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-overview-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Frequency ranking</h4>
                  <p>The table ranks forms by frequency. Use its pager to move beyond the first ten results.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-overview-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Select a form</h4>
                  <p>Click a form in the ranking to make it the focus of the distribution and word-exploration tools.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-overview-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Distribution chart</h4>
                  <p>Shows how the selected form is distributed through page intervals in the dictionary.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-overview-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Length and repetition</h4>
                  <p>Summarises definition length and gives a compact descriptive indication of lexical repetition.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="analysis-explore">
              <h3>Explore words</h3>
              <p>Explore words follows the selected form or group through its contexts and recurrent local patterns.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/analysis-explore-words.png')}
                alt="Annotated Explore words tab with concordances, nearby words and recurring sequences"
                caption="Use the pagers and controls to move through concordances, nearby forms and recurring sequences without leaving the selected word."
                callouts={analysisExploreCallouts}
                leftGutter="118px"
                rightGutter="132px"
                topGutter="76px"
                bottomGutter="82px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Concordances</h4>
                  <p>Shows the selected form in its left and right textual context. The locus link opens the relevant dictionary page and line.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Concordance pager</h4>
                  <p>Use the arrows to browse the complete concordance list in fixed-size pages.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Nearby controls</h4>
                  <p>Choose a ±3 or ±5-token window and optionally hide forms that belong to the focal group itself.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Nearby pager</h4>
                  <p>Browse additional associated forms without expanding the full page vertically.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Sequence settings</h4>
                  <p>Set n-gram length and the minimum numbers of occurrences and entries before running the search.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-6">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">6</span> Run sequence analysis</h4>
                  <p>Press the button after changing the sequence parameters to calculate a new result set.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-explore-7">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">7</span> Sequence results</h4>
                  <p>The table lists recurrent sequences, their counts and examples; use its pager to continue through the ranking.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="analysis-compare">
              <h3>Compare sections</h3>
              <p>Compare sections contrasts the relative frequency of forms in two corpus selections.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/analysis-compare-sections.png')}
                alt="Annotated Compare sections tab with Corpus X and Corpus Y controls"
                caption="Define the two corpora, choose the ranking direction, then browse the complete comparison table."
                callouts={analysisCompareCallouts}
                leftGutter="118px"
                rightGutter="132px"
                topGutter="70px"
                bottomGutter="54px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-compare-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Corpus X</h4>
                  <p>Define the first comparison corpus with its page range and typology.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-compare-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Corpus Y or rest</h4>
                  <p>Define a second corpus manually, or compare X automatically against the rest of the currently filtered dictionary.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-compare-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Ranking views</h4>
                  <p>Switch between forms more frequent in X, more frequent in Y, and forms observed only in one selection.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-compare-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Comparison table</h4>
                  <p>Reports raw counts, normalised frequencies, differences and descriptive ratios for each form.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="analysis-compare-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Result pagination</h4>
                  <p>Use the arrows to move through the full ranking rather than only the first ten forms.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="dictionary">
            <h2>Dictionary</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The Dictionary page is the main environment for browsing the manuscript page by page. It groups entries by page and line,
              exposes the internal structure of each row, and shows contextual details in the right sidebar when the user clicks on a synonym.
            </p>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="dictionary-page">
              <h3>Reading a dictionary page</h3>
              <p>
                The first figure introduces the overall layout of the page-level browser. Use it to understand how page navigation,
                row navigation, filtering, and the contextual sidebar are coordinated.
              </p>

              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/dictionary-page.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the dictionary page layout"
                caption="Annotated example of the page-level dictionary browser. Click a numbered label to jump to the related explanation below."
                callouts={dictionaryPageCallouts}
                leftGutter="118px"
                rightGutter="122px"
                topGutter="76px"
                bottomGutter="68px"
              />

              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Page navigation</h4>
                  <p>The upper controls allow the reader to move backwards and forwards through the manuscript, or to jump directly to a requested page.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Page quick list</h4>
                  <p>The left column summarises the lines currently visible on the selected page. It is a quick page-level navigator rather than a full search index.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Current page</h4>
                  <p>The current page number is always visible and remains the main reference for all loci cited elsewhere in the application.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Main dictionary body</h4>
                  <p>This central area displays the reconstructed content of the manuscript page. Entries are organised by page and line, then expanded into structured columns.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Row-based content</h4>
                  <p>Each visible row may contain one or more readings, variants, glosses, graphic forms and synonym information. The internal logic of a row is explained in the next figure.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-6">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">6</span> Detail sidebar</h4>
                  <p>The right sidebar updates when a synonym or related element is selected. It presents a focused view of the chosen relation and the relevant contextual data.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-page-7">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">7</span> Filters panel</h4>
                  <p>The filter controls refine the current page view. They allow the reader to narrow the visible material without leaving the dictionary browser.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="dictionary-row">
              <h3>Reading a dictionary row</h3>
              <p>
                The second figure focuses on a single row. It is useful for understanding how a manuscript entry is decomposed into fields,
                how variants and alternatives are shown, and how cross-references are activated.
              </p>

              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/dictionary-row.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of a single dictionary row"
                caption="A single row can contain the main reading, alternative readings, definitions, glosses and cross-references."
                callouts={dictionaryRowCallouts}
                leftGutter="128px"
                rightGutter="108px"
                topGutter="70px"
                bottomGutter="62px"
              />

              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Stroke boundary marker</h4>
                  <p>The first narrow column is reserved for the stroke marker. When relevant, it shows the Chinese numeral corresponding to the recorded stroke boundary.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Character and main reading</h4>
                  <p>This block presents the manuscript character together with its primary romanisation. Characters formalised with square brackets are rendered with an asterisk and link to their character record.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Alternative readings</h4>
                  <p>Alternative or secondary readings are grouped beneath the main one. Only alternative readings are repeated here; pure variants are handled in the definitions column.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Definition block</h4>
                  <p>The central definition area keeps the manuscript logic intact: main definition first, then explicitly marked variant definitions when the source distinguishes them.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Glosses</h4>
                  <p>Chinese glosses are shown separately from the Latin definitions, preserving their documentary role inside the original lexicographic structure.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-6">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">6</span> Graphic variants</h4>
                  <p>The graphic variants column isolates alternative graphic forms related to the entry, allowing the reader to compare them at a glance.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="dict-row-7">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">7</span> Synonym cross</h4>
                  <p>Synonyms are arranged in a cross-like layout by position. Clicking one of them opens the sidebar with the preferred scholarly interpretation and the other available options.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="index">
            <h2>Index</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The Index page offers a character-centred exploration of the dictionary. It combines radical-based browsing, text filters,
              a matrix organised by radical and additional strokes, a result table for the selected cell, and a compact analytics bar.
            </p>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="index-overview">
              <h3>General layout</h3>
              <p>The overview figure highlights the main navigational logic of the character index.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/characters-overview.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the general character index layout"
                caption="The character index is organised around a radical × stroke matrix, supplemented by filtering controls and a selected-character sidebar."
                callouts={charactersOverviewCallouts}
                leftGutter="190px"
                rightGutter="132px"
                topGutter="84px"
                bottomGutter="66px"
              />

              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-overview-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Radical browsing controls</h4>
                  <p>The left browsing area combines the radical-mode switch with the radical-family selector. The reader can move between source and modern radical indexing, then choose one of the attested radical families available in the current subset.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-overview-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Filters and quick stats</h4>
                  <p>Historical romanisation, modern romanisation and English meaning can be filtered independently. The active-filter area records the current choices, while the quick statistics summarise the resulting subset.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-overview-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Matrix grid</h4>
                  <p>The matrix crosses radicals with additional strokes. Each cell previews the first attested character in that slot and the total number of attested characters.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-overview-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Selected cell results</h4>
                  <p>When a cell is selected, the result panel below the matrix lists the characters that belong to that combination together with their readings, counts and loci.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-overview-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Selected character panel</h4>
                  <p>The right panel summarises the currently focused character and links the user both to the relevant dictionary loci and to the dedicated character record.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="index-table">
              <h3>Detail of the results table</h3>
              <p>The table translates a selected radical × stroke cell into a browsable list of characters.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/characters-table.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the selected-cell results table"
                caption="Each row in the results table opens a path toward either the dictionary browser or the full character record."
                callouts={charactersTableCallouts}
                leftGutter="116px"
                rightGutter="112px"
                topGutter="72px"
                bottomGutter="64px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-table-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Character preview</h4>
                  <p>The first column displays the character currently indexed for the selected matrix cell.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-table-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Readings and labels</h4>
                  <p>Historical and modern romanisations, together with the relevant labels, allow the reader to compare the documentary and normalised forms.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-table-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> English gloss</h4>
                  <p>The English meaning is meant as a quick orientation tool rather than a substitute for the full Latin definition in the dictionary page or character record.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-table-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Attestation count</h4>
                  <p>This value tells the reader how many attestations are currently associated with the character in the active filtered subset.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-table-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Dictionary loci</h4>
                  <p>The loci buttons open the relevant page and line in the dictionary browser, making it easy to move from the analytical index back to the source layout.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="index-analytics">
              <h3>Detail of the analytics bar</h3>
              <p>The lower analytics area condenses the most useful aggregate views for the current filtered subset.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/characters-analytics.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the lower analytics charts"
                caption="These charts react to the active filters and help the reader understand the distributional profile of the current subset."
                callouts={charactersAnalyticsCallouts}
                topGutter="84px"
                bottomGutter="58px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-analytics-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Attestations over pages</h4>
                  <p>This chart shows how the current set of attestations is distributed across the manuscript pages.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-analytics-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Stroke count distribution</h4>
                  <p>This histogram summarises the stroke counts recorded for the visible subset of characters.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="idx-analytics-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Locus density heatmap</h4>
                  <p>The heatmap aggregates page bins and line bins, making it possible to detect where attestations cluster in the manuscript.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="character-detail">
            <h2>Character detail</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The character record gathers all the information associated with a single entry point in the index. It is the most synthetic view of a character,
              because it combines identity data, lexical relations, documentary evidence, and experimental reconstructions of compounds.
            </p>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="character-top">
              <h3>Upper section</h3>
              <p>The upper part of the page combines navigation, summary data, definitions, and the graph of lexical relations.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/character-detail-top.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the upper part of a character record"
                caption="The upper section is designed for orientation: it identifies the character and immediately exposes its main readings, relations and loci."
                callouts={characterTopCallouts}
                leftGutter="104px"
                rightGutter="132px"
                topGutter="82px"
                bottomGutter="66px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-top-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Section sidebar</h4>
                  <p>The internal sidebar lets the reader jump quickly between overview, readings, lexical relations, variants, compounds, attestation data, and notes.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-top-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Character identity and hero data</h4>
                  <p>The hero area contains the main character form, the core readings, the modern and source radicals, and the relevant stroke counts.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-top-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Readings and definitions table</h4>
                  <p>This table gathers the attested readings, their labels, the English orientation gloss, the Latin definition, and the loci from which the information is drawn.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-top-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Relationship map</h4>
                  <p>The graph visualises lexical relations such as synonyms, variants and compounds, offering a compact conceptual map of the character’s network.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-top-5">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">5</span> Navigation and citation tools</h4>
                  <p>The upper-right controls provide neighbouring-record navigation as well as stable-link and export actions.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-subgroup" id="character-bottom">
              <h3>Lower section</h3>
              <p>The lower half of the page concentrates the denser supporting material: compounds, graphic history, documentary loci, and notes.</p>
              <DocumentationFigure
                src={withBase('/images/chind/documentation/character-detail-bottom.png')}
                alt="Annotated example of the lower part of a character record"
                caption="The lower section stores the most specialised material and makes it traceable back to the documentary evidence."
                callouts={characterBottomCallouts}
                topGutter="76px"
                bottomGutter="68px"
              />
              <div class="doc-notes">
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-bottom-1">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">1</span> Polysyllabic constructions</h4>
                  <p>This panel records disyllabic or polysyllabic constructions associated with the character and serves as the experimental entry point for compound reconstruction.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-bottom-2">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">2</span> Graphic forms and indexing</h4>
                  <p>Here the reader finds the standard and variant forms of the character, radical information, phonetic component, and the indexing data required for search and comparison.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-bottom-3">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">3</span> Documentary evidence</h4>
                  <p>The documentary-evidence table brings together loci, readings, typology, Latin definition, source radical, strokes, and contextual links back to the dictionary browser.</p>
                </section>
                <section class="doc-note" id="char-bottom-4">
                  <h4><span class="doc-step-badge">4</span> Notes and sources</h4>
                  <p>The final panel is reserved for descriptive notes and external references associated with the character record.</p>
                </section>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="doc-section" id="credits">
            <h2>Credits</h2>
            <p class="doc-lead">
              The Credits page lists the research team, the institutional partners, and the project identifiers.
              It is designed as a compact institutional profile of the digital edition and can easily accommodate future profile images or updated roles.
            </p>
            <div class="doc-simple-grid">
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Project roles</h3>
                <p>
                  The central cards are organised hierarchically, distinguishing the Principal Investigator, Project Leads, Content Leads,
                  and the Digital Infrastructure profile.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-simple-card">
                <h3>Institutional frame</h3>
                <p>
                  The lower institutional bar gathers the participating universities, the LAD laboratory, and the formal references to the PRIN PNRR project.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <footer class="doc-footer">
            Documentation page prepared for the local CHIND development environment. All texts are editorial guidance and can be expanded or refined in later stages.
          </footer>
        </article>
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
